// What a missed borrow hoist looks like at run time (ERRATA E23, gap 28).
//
// Jux has no borrow checker a program can fail: aliasing an object and
// mutating it through the alias is ordinary. The backend keeps each borrow of
// an object's cell short (JUX-CLASS-REPRESENTATION §CR.4.1), and where it
// misses one, the program stops on a conflict. That is a compiler bug, and it
// reports itself as one: the Jux type and the Jux line, not Rust's
// `already borrowed: BorrowMutError` at a line of generated Rust.
//
// `fire` calls a field-held lambda that reaches back into the same object,
// and today the field read's borrow is still held during the call. When the
// backend learns to hoist this read, the program prints 1 and this file's
// expected output changes with it.

class Box {
    public int n = 0;
    public () -> void hook = () -> {};

    public void fire() {
        this.hook();
    }

    public void bump() {
        n = n + 1;
    }
}

public void main() {
    Box b = new Box();
    b.hook = () -> b.bump();
    b.fire();
    print(b.n);
}
